Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg
Use RefactorFileIO.pkg
Use RDCRunProgram.pkg          // RDCRunProgramWait - the blocking child-process run
Use cRDCAsyncProcess.pkg         // RDCRunProgramBounded - bounded, no console, real exit code
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg         // rfHtmlEscape and the encoding helpers
/* cLibraryInventoryApp - the JSON-aware reader used to confirm an install actually landed.
   It lives in cFTLibraryReport.pkg, which needs OutputDockFT + FTDashIcons, so this file inherits
   that position in the compile stream. Stated here so moving it too early fails at compile. */
Use cFTLibraryReport.pkg

/*  df-cli PACKAGE PLUMBING - search and install, for the DataFlex 26 package handler.

    DFRefactor DOES NOT IMPLEMENT PACKAGE MANAGEMENT. It drives df-cli, which is the vendor's own
    tool and the only thing that may write the package cache. That line matters: a dependency entry
    naming a package is df-cli's to add and remove, because installing means resolving a version,
    downloading it, unpacking it into the global cache and only THEN writing the .sws. Adding the
    string ourselves would produce a workspace referencing a package that is not there.

    It is also the reason this is not a violation of "DFRefactor fetches nothing" (Nils 2026-07-31,
    about library retargeting): nothing here downloads. df-cli downloads, at the user's request,
    from the package service DataFlex itself ships pointed at.

    BLOCKING, on purpose. Both calls are short, both happen inside a modal dialog the user just
    clicked in, and the async machinery (cRDCAsyncProcess + a poll timer) exists for the compile, which
    is minutes rather than seconds. Its own comment says so.

    Output is captured through a cmd.exe wrapper - the same redirect TestCompileFT uses - because
    RDCRunProgramWait is ShellExecuteEx and cannot capture a pipe.
*/

/*  Where df-cli.exe is, or "" when there is none to be had.

    Derived from the SELECTED COMPILER, which is what defines the target DataFlex version in the
    first place, so the two can never disagree about which release we are talking to. Two shapes:
    the user may have picked df-cli itself, or the DfCompConsole beside it in the same Bin folder.

    df-cli ships with DataFlex 26 and does not exist before it, so a DF25-or-earlier compiler
    correctly yields "" and every caller degrades to saying so. */
Function DfCliPath Global Returns String
    String sComp sTry
    Integer iPos

    Get psCompiler of ghoApplication to sComp
    Move (Trim(sComp)) to sComp
    If (sComp = "") ;
        Function_Return ""
    If (Lowercase(sComp) contains "df-cli") Begin
        If (vFilePathExists(sComp) <> 0) ;
            Function_Return sComp
        Function_Return ""
    End
    Move (RightPos("\", sComp)) to iPos
    If (iPos = 0) ;
        Function_Return ""
    Move (Left(sComp, iPos) + "df-cli.exe") to sTry
    If (vFilePathExists(sTry) <> 0) ;
        Function_Return sTry
    Function_Return ""
End_Function

/*  Runs df-cli with sArgs and returns everything it printed, stdout and stderr together.

    The cmd.exe wrapper is what makes capture possible at all, and its quoting is exact: cmd strips
    the first and last quote of the /c argument, so the real command has to be wrapped in a second
    pair - the same construction StartCompile uses and for the same reason.

    The log lands in DFRefactor's OWN Programs folder: always writable, locale-independent, and
    never inside the workspace being operated on. Overwritten each call. */
/*  The exit code of the LAST DfCliCaptureIn (or DfCliCapture) run.

    A global, because DfCliCapture is a global function - there is no object to hang a property
    on. Read it straight after the call that produced it; nothing else writes it. */
Global_Variable Integer giLastDfCliExit

/*  DfCliCaptureIn: the general form (2026-09-14). sWorkDir is the child's working directory ("" =
    DFRefactor's own Programs folder, the package form); iTimeoutSecs the deadline. The copy step
    converts through this with the copy's home and the convert deadline - a BARE run of df-cli, no
    window and nothing redirected, waits on any failure that would prompt until the deadline kills
    it (probe-measured: 25 s cap, still running, on a corrupt library and on a missing one alike),
    while this wrapper's redirected output makes the same failure exit in 200 ms with its reason in
    the log. DfCliCapture below keeps the package form's signature. */
Function DfCliCaptureIn Global String sArgs String sWorkDir Integer iTimeoutSecs Returns String[]
    String[] asOut asNone
    tSwsFile OwnWS
    String sDfCli sLog sLaunch sLogFolder
    // iRunExit, NOT iExit: iExit is a DataDict FIELD MESSAGE and collides wherever DataDict is
    // in the chain (see the note in cRDCAsyncProcess - this is the second time today).
    Integer iRunExit

    /* A BAIL HERE IS A FAILURE AND SAYS SO (Nils 2026-08-17). Both early exits below used to
       return an empty array with giLastDfCliExit untouched - which on a fresh process is 0, the
       SUCCESS code - so DfCliPackageInstall reported "installed", showed nothing, and Add Package
       "did nothing at all". The cause that day was pOwnSws unparsed (a relative own-.sws name,
       see _OwnSwsInFolder), i.e. the second exit. Now each exit stamps the failed-to-start code
       and returns ONE line naming why, so the caller's "df-cli said:" box carries the reason
       instead of an empty list. */
    Get DfCliPath to sDfCli
    If (sDfCli = "") Begin
        Move CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart to giLastDfCliExit
        Move "df-cli was not started: no df-cli.exe beside the selected compiler." to asNone[0]
        Function_Return asNone
    End

    Get pOwnSws of ghoApplication to OwnWS
    Get PrimaryPrograms of ghoApplication OwnWS to sLogFolder
    If (sLogFolder = "") Begin
        Move CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart to giLastDfCliExit
        Move "df-cli was not started: DFRefactor could not read its own workspace file (pOwnSws), so it has no folder for the df-cli log. Start DFRefactor from its workspace, or report this." to asNone[0]
        Function_Return asNone
    End
    Move (vFolderFormat(sLogFolder) + "dfrefactor_dfcli.log") to sLog
    If (vFilePathExists(sLog)) ;
        Move (rfDeleteFile(sLog)) to sLaunch

    Move ('/c " "' + sDfCli + '" ' + sArgs + ' > "' + sLog + '" 2>&1 "') to sLaunch
    /* BOUNDED, and the EXIT CODE is the point (2026-08-14).

       RDCRunProgramWait waits for ever and reports nothing. A package install clones from a git
       server - minutes, on a big repository - and a private one can ask for credentials, which
       on a hidden console is the deadlock this program spent a day fixing. RDCRunProgramBounded
       gives the child no console and a deadline.

       The exit code matters because the FILE cannot always answer. df-cli is idempotent: asked
       to install something already present it says "already installed and valid ... Added to
       workspace", changes nothing, and is entirely correct - so a caller comparing dependency
       counts before and after sees no change and calls a success a failure. That is exactly what
       Nils hit. The exit code distinguishes them; the counts cannot. */
    // The working directory is the caller's (the copy's own home for a convert - anything df-cli
    // writes relatively then lands there); "" = DFRefactor's own Programs folder, the package form.
    If (Trim(sWorkDir) = "") ;
        Move sLogFolder to sWorkDir
    Send Cursor_Wait of Cursor_Control
    Get RDCRunProgramBounded "cmd.exe" sLaunch sWorkDir iTimeoutSecs to iRunExit
    Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control
    Move iRunExit to giLastDfCliExit

    // No log after the run: the wrapper never got to redirect (failed to start / timed out before
    // df-cli printed). Say which; a run that left no output must not read as a clean one.
    If (vFilePathExists(sLog) = 0) Begin
        If (iRunExit = CI_RDCRunToolTimedOut) ;
            Move ("df-cli did not finish within" * String(iTimeoutSecs) * "seconds and was stopped; it wrote no output.") to asNone[0]
        Else If (iRunExit = CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart) ;
            Move "df-cli could not be started (cmd.exe wrapper failed to launch)." to asNone[0]
        Else ;
            Move ("df-cli exited with code" * String(iRunExit) * "and left no output at" * sLog) to asNone[0]
        Function_Return asNone
    End
    /* EACH LINE AS ITS BYTES ARE (2026-09-30). df-cli's log is MIXED, and a redirect writes no BOM:
       the package registry's text is UTF-8 - E2 80 99 (U+2019) turned "security API's" into three
       mojibake characters when the BOM rule read it as OEM (Browse Packages, Nils 2026-08-21) - but
       df-cli's own messages are in the Windows code page: a workspace path with "åäö" is E5 E4 F6,
       which the UTF-8 read this used to assert turned into replacement characters (measured on an
       Add Package's log, 2026-09-30). rfReadToolOutputLines decodes each line as what it is. The
       ls-remote log below is read the same way - a SHA is ASCII either way, but the two logs are
       written the same way. */
    Move (rfReadToolOutputLines(sLog)) to asOut
    Function_Return asOut
End_Function

// The package form: DFRefactor's own Programs folder as the working directory, the package deadline.
Function DfCliCapture Global String sArgs Returns String[]
    Function_Return (DfCliCaptureIn(sArgs, "", CI_DfCliPackageTimeoutSecs))
End_Function

/*  The packages DataFlex offers for THIS workspace, matching sTerm (blank = everything).

    Parsed from the table df-cli prints:

        Name       | Publisher       | Version    | Description
        DataFlex-dev/Web UI FlexTron | DataFlex-dev    | 1.0.51     | Use Web UI controls ...

    Everything before the header line is skipped - df-cli emits "Config file for project ... using
    defaults." warnings first for any workspace whose projects it cannot fully resolve, which is
    normal and not an error. Rows are taken only AFTER the header, so that noise can never be read
    as a package however it is worded.

    THE SEARCH IS SCOPED BY THE WORKSPACE, not by us: df-cli reads the .sws's own DataFlex version
    and offers only what is compatible with it. That is why the workspace file is a required
    argument and why converting the copy to the target version has to happen first - search a 19.1
    workspace and you correctly get nothing. */
Function DfCliPackageSearch Global String sSwsFile String sTerm Returns tPackageHit[]
    tPackageHit[] aOut
    tPackageHit Hit
    String[] asLines asCols
    String sArgs sLine
    Integer i iMax iRow
    Boolean bPastHeader

    If (sSwsFile = "") ;
        Function_Return aOut
    Move ('package search "' + sSwsFile + '"') to sArgs
    Move (Trim(sTerm)) to sTerm
    If (sTerm <> "") ;
        Move (sArgs + ' "' + sTerm + '"') to sArgs

    Get DfCliCapture sArgs to asLines
    Move False to bPastHeader
    Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sLine
        If (bPastHeader = False) Begin
            // The header, recognised by its own columns rather than by position.
            If (Left(sLine, 4) = "Name" and Pos("|", sLine) > 0 and Pos("Version", sLine) > 0) ;
                Move True to bPastHeader
        End
        Else Begin
            If (Pos("|", sLine) > 0) Begin
                Move (StrSplitToArray(sLine, "|")) to asCols
                If (SizeOfArray(asCols) >= 3) Begin
                    Move (Trim(asCols[0])) to Hit.sName
                    Move (Trim(asCols[1])) to Hit.sPublisher
                    Move (Trim(asCols[2])) to Hit.sVersion
                    Move "" to Hit.sDescription
                    If (SizeOfArray(asCols) >= 4) ;
                        Move (Trim(asCols[3])) to Hit.sDescription
                    // A row with no name or no version cannot be installed, so it is not offered.
                    If (Hit.sName <> "" and Hit.sVersion <> "") Begin
                        Move (SizeOfArray(aOut)) to iRow
                        Move Hit to aOut[iRow]
                    End
                End
            End
        End
    Loop
    Function_Return aOut
End_Function

/*  Installs sPackage into sSwsFile through df-cli, which writes the dependency itself.

    THE .sws IS DF-CLI'S TO EDIT HERE, and that is the whole point. Install resolves the version,
    downloads it, unpacks it into the global cache and then adds the entry - so a caller holding its
    own in-memory copy of the dependency list must RE-READ the file afterwards rather than write its
    version over the top. See DoAddPackage, which saves first and reloads after for exactly this
    reason.

    Verified by re-reading the file, never by an exit code: the question is whether the entry is
    there now. sOutput carries what df-cli said, so a failure can be shown rather than summarised.

    bAdded (2026-08-17) tells a SUCCESS apart from an ALREADY-THERE: df-cli is idempotent and exits 0
    for a package the workspace already lists ("already installed and valid ... Added to workspace"),
    changing nothing - so True with bAdded False is "nothing to do", which the caller must SAY.
    Nils: re-adding vwin32fh "was silence and nothing happened", and pasting an example that was
    already installed looked like a dead OK button. Judged by the FILE (did the entry count rise). */
Function DfCliPackageInstall Global String sSwsFile String sPackage String[] ByRef asOutput Boolean ByRef bAdded Returns Boolean
    String[] asLines asBefore asAfter
    String sArgs
    Handle hoInv
    Integer iBefore iAfter iObjBefore iObjAfter

    Move False to bAdded
    Move (Trim(sPackage)) to sPackage
    If (sSwsFile = "" or sPackage = "") ;
        Function_Return False

    /* COUNT BOTH KINDS OF DEPENDENCY (Nils 2026-08-17, fresh WinServ copy: the vwin32fh git package
       was installed - the .sws had it at once - and the dialog said "already in the workspace's
       dependency list, so nothing was added"). SwsLibraryEntries lists the PATH entries only; a
       system or git package is an OBJECT entry, so its arrival never moved that count. Sum both. */
    Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
    Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSwsFile to asBefore
    Get SwsJsonObjectDependencyCount of hoInv sSwsFile to iObjBefore
    Move (SizeOfArray(asBefore) + iObjBefore) to iBefore

    Move ('package install "' + sSwsFile + '" "' + sPackage + '"') to sArgs
    Get DfCliCapture sArgs to asLines
    Move asLines to asOutput

    Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSwsFile to asAfter
    Get SwsJsonObjectDependencyCount of hoInv sSwsFile to iObjAfter
    Move (SizeOfArray(asAfter) + iObjAfter) to iAfter
    Send Destroy of hoInv
    /* THE EXIT CODE FIRST, the count only as a fallback (2026-08-14).

       df-cli is idempotent: install something already in the workspace and it says "already
       installed and valid ... Added to workspace", changes nothing, and is right to. Counting
       dependencies before and after then reads a success as a failure, which is what Nils saw
       after his first (successful) attempt had already added the package.

       The count is kept as a second opinion for the case where the exit code is unavailable -
       CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart or a timeout - because a list that GREW is proof either way. */
    Move (iAfter > iBefore) to bAdded
    Function_Return (giLastDfCliExit = 0 or bAdded = True)
End_Function

/*  Uninstalls sPackage from sSwsFile through df-cli, which removes the dependency itself.

    THE SAME RULE AS INSTALL, and it is the half that is easy to get wrong: dropping the string from
    the dependency array by hand LOOKS like an uninstall. It is not. The cache entry stays, the
    package's own transitive dependencies stay, and - the part that actually bites - df-cli's check
    that nothing else still depends on it never runs.

    NO --force. df-cli refuses when another package depends on this one, and that refusal is the
    most useful thing it can say; forcing past it by default would turn a protection into a
    formality. The caller shows df-cli's own words, which name the dependent.

    Verified by re-reading: the entry count has to have gone DOWN. An exit code cannot distinguish
    "removed" from "there was nothing to remove". */
Function DfCliPackageUninstall Global String sSwsFile String sPackage String[] ByRef asOutput Returns Boolean
    String[] asLines asBefore asAfter
    String sArgs
    Handle hoInv
    Integer iBefore iAfter

    Move (Trim(sPackage)) to sPackage
    If (sSwsFile = "" or sPackage = "") ;
        Function_Return False

    Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
    Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSwsFile to asBefore
    Move (SizeOfArray(asBefore)) to iBefore

    Move ('package uninstall "' + sSwsFile + '" "' + sPackage + '"') to sArgs
    Get DfCliCapture sArgs to asLines
    Move asLines to asOutput

    Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSwsFile to asAfter
    Move (SizeOfArray(asAfter)) to iAfter
    Send Destroy of hoInv
    // Same reasoning as the install: df-cli owns the verdict, the count is the fallback.
    Function_Return (giLastDfCliExit = 0 or iAfter < iBefore)
End_Function
Define CI_GitLsRemoteTimeoutSecs for 20   // one remote head, no clone - short on purpose
/*  THE AUTOMATIC path gets a much shorter bound than the button. A user who pressed Update Check
    is waiting on purpose and 20 seconds of a hung remote is tolerable; a check nobody asked for,
    fired because a workspace was opened, must not be able to sit on the UI thread. Off the network
    ls-remote does not fail fast - it blocks until the bound - and that cost is per git dependency,
    serially. Four seconds is comfortably above a normal round trip (measured well under one) and
    low enough that the worst case is a hesitation rather than a hang. */
Define CI_GitLsRemoteQuickSecs for 4

/*  Run `git ls-remote <sArgs>` bounded and console-less, and hand back what it printed.

    ONE HOME for the launch since 2026-09-25, when a second question joined the first: the branch
    head (GitRemoteHeadEx) and the published tags (GitRemoteTagsEx) go out the same way - the same
    log file in the workspace's Programs folder, the same deadline, the same line-by-line read
    (rfReadToolOutputLines).
    Returns RDCRunProgramBounded's answer, git's exit code - and CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart when no log
    appeared, so an EMPTY answer (a repository with no tags) and NO answer never look alike. */
Function GitLsRemoteLines Global String sArgs Integer iTimeoutSecs String[] ByRef asOut Returns Integer
    String[] asNone
    String sLog sLaunch sLogFolder
    tSwsFile OwnWS
    Integer iRunExit

    Move asNone to asOut
    Get pOwnSws of ghoApplication to OwnWS
    Get PrimaryPrograms of ghoApplication OwnWS to sLogFolder
    If (sLogFolder = "") ;
        Function_Return CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart
    Move (vFolderFormat(sLogFolder) + "dfrefactor_lsremote.log") to sLog
    If (vFilePathExists(sLog)) ;
        Move (rfDeleteFile(sLog)) to sLaunch

    Move ('/c " git ls-remote ' + sArgs + ' > "' + sLog + '" 2>&1 "') to sLaunch
    Send Cursor_Wait of Cursor_Control
    Get RDCRunProgramBounded "cmd.exe" sLaunch sLogFolder iTimeoutSecs to iRunExit
    Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control

    If (vFilePathExists(sLog) = 0) ;
        Function_Return CI_RDCRunToolFailedToStart
    Move (rfReadToolOutputLines(sLog)) to asOut
    Function_Return iRunExit
End_Function

/*  The commit a git remote's HEAD points at, WITHOUT cloning it.

    `git ls-remote <url> HEAD` prints one line - "<40-char sha><TAB>HEAD".

    THIS COMMAND HAS ONLY ONE BRANCH: it always prints the remote head and never reads our pin, so
    "is the pin stale?" is answered by comparing this to the .sws version IN OUR CODE, not by the
    command behaving differently. That is the whole reason it is safe where `package update` is not
    - see feedback_dfcli_package_update_is_not_a_check: `update` says it "checks", but on a
    workspace whose pins differ it AUTO-INSTALLS and can leave the workspace BROKEN (exit 15).
    Proven read-only 2026-08-20 against a 297-entry fingerprint of the whole workspace: ls-remote
    takes no workspace argument and wrote nothing.

    Bounded and console-less for the reason df-cli is: a PRIVATE repo can ask for credentials, and a
    hidden console with a prompt is a deadlock. A repo that asks returns "" here - which the caller
    must read as UNKNOWN, never as "up to date". */
/*  The remote branch head, with the caller naming its own patience.

    Split out 2026-09-10 for the tile's automatic check: same body, and the four-argument name
    below still passes CI_GitLsRemoteTimeoutSecs, so the Libraries dialog and every other caller
    are untouched. Same shape as rfReadEncodedFileLines / rfWriteEncodedFileFromStringArrayEx -
    an Ex form carries the new argument and the old name keeps the old default. */
Function GitRemoteHeadEx Global String sUrl Integer iTimeoutSecs Returns String
    String[] asOut
    String sLine
    Integer iRunExit iPos

    Move (Trim(sUrl)) to sUrl
    If (sUrl = "") ;
        Function_Return ""
    Get GitLsRemoteLines ('"' + sUrl + '" HEAD') iTimeoutSecs (&asOut) to iRunExit
    If (SizeOfArray(asOut) = 0) ;
        Function_Return ""

    Move (Trim(asOut[0])) to sLine
    // "<sha><TAB>HEAD". Cut at the tab (or a space, if the shell normalised it). A git ERROR
    // message has neither shape, and the length test below rejects it.
    Move (Pos(Character(9), sLine)) to iPos
    If (iPos = 0) ;
        Move (Pos(" ", sLine)) to iPos
    If (iPos > 1) ;
        Move (Left(sLine, iPos - 1)) to sLine
    If (Length(sLine) <> 40) ;
        Function_Return ""
    Function_Return (Lowercase(sLine))
End_Function

/*  The version tags a git remote has published, WITHOUT cloning it (2026-09-25).

    `git ls-remote --tags --refs <url>`: the tags alone, without their peeled "^{}" twins, read by
    GitTagsFromLsRemote (cLibraryInventory.pkg, where the console runner can test it). It is the
    question a TAG pin needs answered, as GitRemoteHeadEx answers it for a commit.

    False is UNKNOWN - git missing, the network down, the deadline passed, a remote asking for
    credentials, or output of any other shape - and must never be read as "nothing newer". True
    with no tags is a real answer: the repository has published none. Same bounds as the head
    question, and just as read-only: ls-remote takes no workspace and writes nothing but its log. */
Function GitRemoteTagsEx Global String sUrl Integer iTimeoutSecs String[] ByRef asTags Returns Boolean
    String[] asOut asNone
    Integer iRunExit

    Move asNone to asTags
    Move (Trim(sUrl)) to sUrl
    If (sUrl = "") ;
        Function_Return False
    Get GitLsRemoteLines ('--tags --refs "' + sUrl + '"') iTimeoutSecs (&asOut) to iRunExit
    // Git's own words on a failure are in asOut - they are not tags, and not "no tags" either.
    If (iRunExit <> 0) ;
        Function_Return False
    Function_Return (GitTagsFromLsRemote(asOut, &asTags))
End_Function
/*  ASK WHAT IS NEWER, AND CHANGE NOTHING. The one implementation behind both the Libraries
    dialog's Update Check button and the Libraries tile's automatic check (2026-09-10).

    ONE HOME, because two callers need it and they must not disagree - the same reason
    ExpandPackageGraph has one. A tile saying "up to date" while the dialog's chips say otherwise
    is the exact failure a second copy of this loop produces, and this codebase has had that bug
    before with the library counts.

    Two sources, because the two kinds of dependency answer to different things:

      system  -> DfCliPackageSearch, which df-cli scopes BY THE WORKSPACE'S DataFlex version.
                  `package details` also reports a version but is UNSCOPED - measured, it offered
                  1.1.0 for a theme whose compatible newest was 1.0.4 - so an Update badge driven
                  by details would point the user at an incompatible release.
      git     -> by what the pin IS (2026-09-25): a commit against the branch head, a version tag
                  against the newest tag a caret on it accepts. A range or a branch is not asked.

    NEVER `package update`. Its help says "Checks for an update" and it takes no --yes, which reads
    as safe; measured 2026-08-20 it AUTO-INSTALLS whenever the pins differ from what is installed,
    and left a probe workspace BROKEN at exit 15. Doing that to a workspace because the user merely
    OPENED it would be far worse than doing it from a button labelled "check".

    iAnswered IS NOT A COUNT OF PROBLEMS, it is how many dependencies gave a definite answer. It
    exists so a caller can tell "checked, nothing behind" from "could not check" - which look
    identical in the return value and must never read the same on screen. GitRemoteHeadEx returns
    "" for a missing git, a down network or a remote asking for credentials, and that is UNKNOWN,
    never up-to-date. search EXITS 15 on a workspace whose pins have drifted: safe, but no rows.

    Returns the number of dependencies with something newer. Local path libraries are not counted
    in either figure - they have no remote to ask. */
Function WorkspaceUpdateScan Global String sSwsFile Integer iTimeoutSecs String[] ByRef asKey ;
                             String[] ByRef asLatest Integer ByRef iCliExit ;
                             Integer ByRef iAnswered Returns Integer
    tPackageHit[] aHits
    tSwsDependency[] aObjDeps
    String[] asEntries asKeyOut asLatestOut asEmpty asTags
    String sEntry sName sPinned sHead sNewer sRepo
    Boolean bTags
    Integer i j iMax jMax iFound iAns iPkgRows
    Handle hoInv

    Move asEmpty to asKey
    Move asEmpty to asLatest
    Move 0 to iCliExit
    Move 0 to iAnswered
    Move 0 to iFound
    Move 0 to iAns
    If (Trim(sSwsFile) = "" or DfCliPath() = "") ;
        Function_Return 0

    Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to hoInv
    Get SwsLibraryEntries of hoInv sSwsFile to asEntries
    Get SwsJsonObjectDependencies of hoInv sSwsFile to aObjDeps

    /* THE REGISTRY SEARCH IS SKIPPED WHEN NOTHING NEEDS IT. A df-cli run is a process spawn plus a
       call to the registry, and the everyday workspace this tool opens declares only path libraries
       and git dependencies - for those the search can answer nothing and would be pure cost. */
    Move 0 to iPkgRows
    Move (SizeOfArray(asEntries) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Get PackageVersion of hoInv (Trim(asEntries[i])) to sPinned
        If (sPinned <> "") ;
            Increment iPkgRows
    Loop

    If (iPkgRows > 0) Begin
        Get DfCliPackageSearch sSwsFile "" to aHits
        Move giLastDfCliExit to iCliExit
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(asEntries[i])) to sEntry
            Get PackageVersion of hoInv sEntry to sPinned
            If (sPinned <> "") Begin
                Get PackageRepository of hoInv sEntry to sRepo
                If (sRepo <> "") ;
                    Move (sRepo + "/") to sRepo
                Get PackageName of hoInv sEntry to sName
                Move (sRepo + sName) to sName
                Move (SizeOfArray(aHits) - 1) to jMax
                For j from 0 to jMax
                    If (Uppercase(Trim(aHits[j].sName)) = Uppercase(sName)) Begin
                        Increment iAns
                        If (Trim(aHits[j].sVersion) <> sPinned) Begin
                            Move sEntry                    to asKeyOut[iFound]
                            Move (Trim(aHits[j].sVersion)) to asLatestOut[iFound]
                            Increment iFound
                        End
                    End
                Loop
            End
        Loop
    End

    /* Git rows, by what the pin IS (2026-09-25 - IsGitCommitPin in cLibraryInventory.pkg):
         a commit      -> the branch head, as it always was;
         a version tag -> the newest published tag a caret on the pin still accepts, so the offer
                          is one the workspace's libraries take - never a new major;
         anything else -> a range or a branch has no one pinned version to compare, so it is left
                          out of both counts. Before this a branch pin ALWAYS read as behind its own
                          head, and Update then wrote that commit over the branch name. */
    Move (SizeOfArray(aObjDeps) - 1) to iMax
    For i from 0 to iMax
        Move (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sVersion)) to sPinned
        Move "" to sNewer
        If (IsGitCommitPin(sPinned) = True) Begin
            Get GitRemoteHeadEx (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource)) iTimeoutSecs to sHead
            If (sHead <> "") Begin
                Increment iAns
                If (Lowercase(sPinned) <> sHead) ;
                    Move sHead to sNewer
            End
        End
        Else If (IsVersionTagPin(sPinned) = True) Begin
            Get GitRemoteTagsEx (Trim(aObjDeps[i].sSource)) iTimeoutSecs (&asTags) to bTags
            If (bTags = True) Begin
                Increment iAns
                Move (NewestCompatibleVersionTag(sPinned, asTags)) to sNewer
            End
        End
        If (sNewer <> "") Begin
            Get SwsDependencyKey aObjDeps[i] to sName
            If (sName <> "") Begin
                Move sName  to asKeyOut[iFound]
                Move sNewer to asLatestOut[iFound]
                Increment iFound
            End
        End
    Loop
    Send Destroy of hoInv

    Move asKeyOut    to asKey
    Move asLatestOut to asLatest
    Move iAns        to iAnswered
    Function_Return iFound
End_Function

/*  Is the automatic check wanted? Default ON, and it is a plain switch rather than a "how often":
    the scan already runs at most once per workspace per session and skips a workspace with no
    remote-backed dependency at all, so there is nothing an interval would save that is worth a
    second thing to explain. Anyone who wants it off is on a machine where it cannot answer.

    Read every time rather than cached, because the cost is one settings read against a scan that
    spawns processes - see the rule that a freshness check must be cheaper than what it guards. */
Function PackageUpdateCheckEnabled Global Returns Boolean
    Integer iVal
    Get ReadInteger of ghoApplication CS_Settings CS_CheckPackageUpdates True to iVal
    Function_Return (iVal <> 0)
End_Function

/*  THE BOARDS' ONE LINE: scan, and leave the verdict where the Libraries tile will find it.

    On the QUICK bound, because this runs because a workspace was opened and not because anyone
    asked. The answer is published even when nothing could be reached - bAnswered False - so the
    board stops asking for this workspace and the tile stays silent rather than claiming anything.

    Returns the number behind, which no caller needs but makes the function testable by hand. */
Function PublishWorkspaceUpdateScan Global String sSwsFile Returns Integer
    String[] asKey asLatest
    Integer iFound iErr iAnswered

    Move (Trim(sSwsFile)) to sSwsFile
    If (sSwsFile = "") ;
        Function_Return 0
    Get WorkspaceUpdateScan sSwsFile CI_GitLsRemoteQuickSecs (&asKey) (&asLatest) (&iErr) (&iAnswered) to iFound
    Send SetUpdateResult of ghoLibraryReport sSwsFile iFound (iAnswered > 0)
    Function_Return iFound
End_Function

/*  Upgrade ONE package to the newest version its workspace allows.

    `--yes` IS NOT OPTIONAL. Without it df-cli prompts for confirmation, and a bounded console-less
    child that prompts is exactly the deadlock RDCRunProgramBounded exists to avoid - DFRefactor
    would hang with no output until the timeout. The user has already confirmed in our own dialog.

    Named package only. `upgrade` with no package upgrades EVERYTHING, and pins are interdependent
    (measured: "Incompatible version for DataFlex-dev/Web UI: 1.0.51 does not satisfy 1.0.52"), so
    a blanket upgrade can move packages the user never looked at. The caller passes one name. */
Function DfCliPackageUpgrade Global String sSwsFile String sPackage String[] ByRef asOutput Returns Boolean
    String[] asLines
    String sArgs

    Move (Trim(sPackage)) to sPackage
    If (sSwsFile = "" or sPackage = "") ;
        Function_Return False

    Move ('package upgrade --yes "' + sSwsFile + '" "' + sPackage + '"') to sArgs
    Get DfCliCapture sArgs to asLines
    Move asLines to asOutput
    // df-cli owns the verdict. There is no count to fall back on: an upgrade changes a version
    // string in place, so the dependency COUNT is identical either way.
    Function_Return (giLastDfCliExit = 0)
End_Function
/*  Make the INSTALLED set match the pins, for one named package.

    This is `package update`, the command [[feedback_dfcli_package_update_is_not_a_check]] warns
    must never be used as a CHECK - because whenever the pins differ from what is installed it
    auto-installs, and on a drifted workspace it can end BROKEN at exit 15.

    Here that behaviour is exactly the point and is invoked deliberately, immediately AFTER we have
    rewritten a git dependency's pin: the pins are now what the user asked for, and this makes the
    checkout agree with them. Never call it speculatively; never call it to find out whether
    something is newer. */
Function DfCliPackageReconcile Global String sSwsFile String sPackage String[] ByRef asOutput Returns Boolean
    String[] asLines
    String sArgs

    Move (Trim(sPackage)) to sPackage
    If (sSwsFile = "") ;
        Function_Return False

    If (sPackage = "") ;
        Move ('package update "' + sSwsFile + '"') to sArgs
    Else ;
        Move ('package update "' + sSwsFile + '" "' + sPackage + '"') to sArgs
    Get DfCliCapture sArgs to asLines
    Move asLines to asOutput
    Function_Return (giLastDfCliExit = 0)
End_Function
Define CI_HttpGetTimeoutSecs for 20   // one small document; a package list must not hang on it

/*  Fetch one small text document over HTTPS, into a string array.

    DFRefactor's FIRST http call, and deliberately the same shape as everything else that leaves the
    process: curl.exe (shipped with Windows since 1803) run through RDCRunProgramBounded - no console,
    a deadline, output redirected to a file we then read. No new dependency, no COM object, and the
    same deadlock protection df-cli and ls-remote already rely on.

    ONLY EVER ON DEMAND. This is why package details are a popup and not a card: a fetch per row
    would put the network between the user and a list that must open instantly. The caller is a
    click.

    Read as UTF-8 without asking for a BOM - a downloaded document has none, and the package service
    returns UTF-8 (see feedback_dfcli_package_update_is_not_a_check's sibling lesson: the same
    mistake mangled an apostrophe in Browse Packages).

    -sS = quiet but still report errors; -L follows a redirect; --max-time bounds it inside curl as
    well as outside. Returns an empty array on any failure - the caller says so rather than showing
    half a document. */
Function rfHttpGetText Global String sUrl Returns String[]
    String[] asOut asNone
    String sLog sLaunch sLogFolder
    tSwsFile OwnWS
    Integer iRunExit

    Move (Trim(sUrl)) to sUrl
    If (sUrl = "") ;
        Function_Return asNone
    // http/https only, the same refusal RDCOpenUrl makes - this runs a program with the string in it.
    If (Lowercase(Left(sUrl, 8)) <> "https://" and Lowercase(Left(sUrl, 7)) <> "http://") ;
        Function_Return asNone

    Get pOwnSws of ghoApplication to OwnWS
    Get PrimaryPrograms of ghoApplication OwnWS to sLogFolder
    If (sLogFolder = "") ;
        Function_Return asNone
    Move (vFolderFormat(sLogFolder) + "dfrefactor_http.txt") to sLog
    If (vFilePathExists(sLog)) ;
        Move (rfDeleteFile(sLog)) to sLaunch

    Move ('/c " curl.exe -sS -L --max-time ' + String(CI_HttpGetTimeoutSecs) ;
          + ' "' + sUrl + '" > "' + sLog + '" 2>&1 "') to sLaunch
    Send Cursor_Wait of Cursor_Control
    Get RDCRunProgramBounded "cmd.exe" sLaunch sLogFolder CI_HttpGetTimeoutSecs to iRunExit
    Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control

    If (vFilePathExists(sLog) = 0) ;
        Function_Return asNone
    Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArrayEx(sLog, True)) to asOut

    /* DELETED ONCE READ, unlike the df-cli and ls-remote logs beside it. Those are diagnostics worth
       keeping after a failed call; this is a response BODY, already in asOut and of no use on disk -
       it can run to any size the server likes, and it lands in the USER'S Programs folder, where it
       turned up as an untracked file in the repo (2026-08-21). Deleting on the way in only, as the
       first cut did, always leaves the last one behind. */
    If (vFilePathExists(sLog)) ;
        Move (rfDeleteFile(sLog)) to sLaunch
    Function_Return asOut
End_Function
